[38;2;124;124;124mstatements are Π₁).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, there's a general principle: if T₂ is an extension of T₁ (T₂ ⊇ T₁), then [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mCon(T₂) → Con(T₁) is provable in very weak systems, like PA (Peano Arithmetic), [0m[38;2;124;124;124mor even weaker.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe reason: if T₁ is inconsistent, there's a finite proof of a contradiction [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfrom T₁. Since T₂ extends T₁, that same proof is a proof of a contradiction from[0m[38;2;124;124;124mT₂. So ZFC (and even PA) can prove: "if T₁ has a proof of contradiction, then T₂[0m[38;2;124;124;124mhas a proof of contradiction," which is the contrapositive: ¬Con(T₁) → ¬Con(T₂),[0m[38;2;124;124;124mi.e., Con(T₂) → Con(T₁).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a finitary argument — it only involves checking that a finite proof in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mT₁ is also a valid proof in T₂, which requires only checking that every axiom of[0m[38;2;124;124;124mT₁ is an axiom of T₂. This is a primitive recursive check.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo yes, ZFC can prove Con(ZFC + Con(ZFC)) → Con(ZFC), because ZFC + Con(ZFC) [0m
